1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,860
So we've got the National Assembly, which is the conflation of what had previously been the three estates.

2
00:00:04,900 --> 00:00:09,600
There is now the National Assembly is the embodiment of the will and the sovereignty of the people.

3
00:00:10,720 --> 00:00:15,900
The Bastille has fallen. The first émigrés have fled.

4
00:00:16,900 --> 00:00:23,040
And Louis, who has not fled, is trying to reinvent himself as a constitutional monarch.

5
00:00:23,560 --> 00:00:28,780
But while all this is going on, everything that precipitated the revolution,

6
00:00:28,780 --> 00:00:35,120
so chiefly the fact that there's no money and that the bread price is very high, that hasn't really changed.

7
00:00:35,520 --> 00:00:38,480
Yeah. So there's still an atmosphere of panic and crisis, isn't there?

8
00:00:39,140 --> 00:00:43,260
So here's the thing. Why are the deputies who are in the National Assembly,

9
00:00:43,580 --> 00:00:49,280
why would they spend all their time talking about a declaration of human rights

10
00:00:49,280 --> 00:00:57,640
when you would think their priority would be to be discussing bread supplies or whatever it might be?

11
00:00:57,760 --> 00:00:58,140
Because.

12
00:00:58,780 --> 00:01:03,940
I think that most of the deputies who had assembled at Versailles in May 1789,

13
00:01:04,500 --> 00:01:07,060
say for the Estates General, that then becomes the National Assembly.

14
00:01:07,260 --> 00:01:10,380
I mean, they thought that they had come there to give France a new constitution.

15
00:01:10,880 --> 00:01:14,960
And I think they operate on the assumption that you can't remedy the ills facing France

16
00:01:14,960 --> 00:01:18,880
unless you have set the country on new foundations.

17
00:01:19,280 --> 00:01:28,420
And the Declaration of Rights is an attempt to frame for both the delegates,

18
00:01:28,420 --> 00:01:30,600
themselves, but also for all the people of France,

19
00:01:30,760 --> 00:01:36,540
an understanding of what henceforward will be animating the new France.

20
00:01:36,800 --> 00:01:36,920
Right.

21
00:01:37,140 --> 00:01:41,240
And I think that that is actually an understanding that is turbocharged by the fall of the Bastille.

22
00:01:41,620 --> 00:01:46,140
Because you were saying how the people in the National Assembly are actually perturbed by what happens.

23
00:01:46,360 --> 00:01:46,600
Yeah.

24
00:01:46,800 --> 00:01:47,800
They're unsettled by it.

25
00:01:47,960 --> 00:01:51,240
And actually, I'm right in saying, aren't I, that as the weeks go by,

26
00:01:51,380 --> 00:01:53,340
so during the time they're thinking about this,

27
00:01:53,480 --> 00:01:56,940
the news coming in, not so much from Paris, but from the countryside,

28
00:01:57,140 --> 00:01:57,560
Yeah.

29
00:01:57,780 --> 00:01:58,380
is more.

30
00:01:58,380 --> 00:02:02,680
Alarming than ever, and arguably more alarming than ever before in French history,

31
00:02:02,840 --> 00:02:05,800
because this is the year of what historians call the great fear,

32
00:02:05,820 --> 00:02:07,700
la grande peur, the great fear.

33
00:02:07,780 --> 00:02:08,100
The great fear.

34
00:02:08,200 --> 00:02:09,760
So this is coming in from the provinces.

35
00:02:10,280 --> 00:02:10,420
Yeah.

36
00:02:10,500 --> 00:02:13,040
I mean, I think there's hope as well as fear.

37
00:02:14,180 --> 00:02:22,040
So there's, we've already mentioned him, Arthur Young, this Englishman who's gone on a trip across France.

38
00:02:22,200 --> 00:02:23,540
An ill-timed holiday.

39
00:02:23,560 --> 00:02:24,480
Yeah, an ill-timed trip.

40
00:02:24,780 --> 00:02:28,360
So he, two days before, he went on a trip to Paris.

41
00:02:28,360 --> 00:02:32,220
Before the fall of the Bastille, he is in Lorraine and he meets a peasant woman.

42
00:02:32,360 --> 00:02:34,200
He asks her, you know, how you doing?

43
00:02:34,400 --> 00:02:35,920
And he writes what she said.

44
00:02:36,020 --> 00:02:40,340
It was said at present that something was to be done by some great folks for such poor ones,

45
00:02:40,480 --> 00:02:45,500
but she did not know who nor how, but God send us better because property taxes and

46
00:02:45,500 --> 00:02:47,360
feudal dues are crushing us.

47
00:02:47,700 --> 00:02:51,640
And Arthur Young, as a kind of illustration of the way that the poor are being crushed

48
00:02:51,640 --> 00:02:58,000
by the weight on them, he assumes that this woman must be kind of 60 or 70, but he asks,

49
00:02:58,000 --> 00:02:59,420
and she turns out to be 28.

50
00:03:00,920 --> 00:03:01,800
So, yeah.

51
00:03:02,440 --> 00:03:05,160
Oh no, that's how French people look though, Tom, what can you do?

52
00:03:05,280 --> 00:03:12,360
But then the Bastille falls and that actually, that turns up, it doesn't lessen the temperature

53
00:03:12,360 --> 00:03:12,900
in the countryside.

54
00:03:13,120 --> 00:03:19,080
It actually, you know, it sends it through the roof because people now start, you know,

55
00:03:19,080 --> 00:03:24,280
they hear that one fortress has fallen and they start to attack other citadels of authority,

56
00:03:24,500 --> 00:03:24,820
don't they?

57
00:03:24,840 --> 00:03:27,980
And they're burning the registers and they're smashing their way in.

58
00:03:28,000 --> 00:03:31,980
I think the fall of the Bastille is reported as the fall of feudalism.

59
00:03:32,320 --> 00:03:36,440
And we, again, mentioned this in the previous episode, it's very peasants revolt.

60
00:03:36,780 --> 00:03:42,780
There's this idea that if you as a peasant can burn the registers and the charters that

61
00:03:42,780 --> 00:03:45,760
condemn you to work as a peasant, then you will be free.

62
00:03:45,980 --> 00:03:53,540
And so they start targeting castes, yeah, chateaux and particularly abbeys and monasteries,

63
00:03:53,540 --> 00:03:57,900
which also, you know, as in the days of the peasant revolt, own large tracts of land,

64
00:03:57,900 --> 00:04:01,740
and this brings kind of feudal dues and tithes with them.

65
00:04:02,430 --> 00:04:09,580
So an example of an abbey that gets attacked is one in Burgundy called Senazon, and this

66
00:04:09,580 --> 00:04:15,000
is completely leveled and it belongs to a very distinguished local family, the family

67
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:20,260
of Talleyrand-Périgord, and the best known member of this family is actually a bishop,

68
00:04:20,380 --> 00:04:27,880
the bishop of the local town of Autun, and he had basically become a bishop because his

69
00:04:27,880 --> 00:04:32,140
nurse had not been looking after him and drops him on the floor and it damages his

70
00:04:32,140 --> 00:04:35,220
foot when he's about three and the foot never heals.

71
00:04:35,400 --> 00:04:41,120
And so he grows up unable to fulfill the military destiny that otherwise would have been his

72
00:04:41,120 --> 00:04:43,140
as a kind of swaggering aristocrat.

73
00:04:43,660 --> 00:04:49,440
And so he has no choice but to go into the church, which is an absolute mismatch between

74
00:04:49,440 --> 00:04:52,920
his talents because he's quite marquis de Sade.

75
00:04:53,100 --> 00:04:55,020
I mean, he's not that bad, but he's a kind of cynic.

76
00:04:55,060 --> 00:04:56,200
He's a free thinker.

77
00:04:56,360 --> 00:04:57,300
He is.

78
00:04:57,300 --> 00:04:57,860
He's done.

79
00:04:57,860 --> 00:05:00,740
Definitely inclined to libertinism.

80
00:05:00,780 --> 00:05:01,900
I think it's fair to say.

81
00:05:02,020 --> 00:05:02,340
Yeah.

82
00:05:02,660 --> 00:05:10,520
But he is unbelievably able, shrewd, kind of cunning operator, kind of almost the embodiment

83
00:05:10,520 --> 00:05:11,900
of the Machiavelli, isn't he?

84
00:05:12,380 --> 00:05:13,940
Yeah, I mean, he's the great survivor.

85
00:05:14,040 --> 00:05:14,700
The great survivor.

86
00:05:15,040 --> 00:05:19,820
So he gets elected as a member of the first estate, so that's the church, to go to Versailles.

87
00:05:20,800 --> 00:05:26,820
And when he's there, rather than kind of bat for the church, he teams up with Mirabeau,

88
00:05:26,940 --> 00:05:27,640
who's the great

89
00:05:27,640 --> 00:05:28,720
spokesman for the third estate.

90
00:05:29,120 --> 00:05:36,140
And they are sat together, kind of working out drafts for the Declaration of the Rights

91
00:05:36,140 --> 00:05:39,380
of Man as news from the provinces is coming in.

92
00:05:39,600 --> 00:05:46,920
And the news is very, very alarming if you have any commitment to the integrity of the

93
00:05:46,920 --> 00:05:47,220
nation.

94
00:05:47,420 --> 00:05:49,660
So the whole country seems to be disintegrating.

95
00:05:49,700 --> 00:05:55,060
And we talked about the importance of rumour and the anxiety of conspiracy in the context

96
00:05:55,060 --> 00:05:55,800
of the Bastille.

97
00:05:55,860 --> 00:05:57,540
This is exactly what's happening in the country.

98
00:05:57,540 --> 00:06:04,360
And there are kind of terrified stories that the aristocrats are deliberately setting out

99
00:06:04,360 --> 00:06:06,340
to starve or poison the poor.

100
00:06:06,820 --> 00:06:12,380
And if that sounds mad, again, this is a staple of plots in the Marquis de Sade's novels.

101
00:06:12,760 --> 00:06:18,140
This idea that aristocrats have nothing better to do than to kind of plot starvation and

102
00:06:18,140 --> 00:06:22,580
ruin for the entire country is clearly part of the kind of the climate of paranoia of

103
00:06:22,580 --> 00:06:23,000
the time.

104
00:06:23,220 --> 00:06:25,960
And even at this stage, there's a sort of...

105
00:06:26,520 --> 00:06:27,160
There's a...

106
00:06:27,160 --> 00:06:27,520
There's a...

107
00:06:27,540 --> 00:06:31,820
I don't know whether xenophobia is too strong a word, but there's a great fear of foreigners,

108
00:06:31,940 --> 00:06:32,300
isn't there?

109
00:06:32,340 --> 00:06:36,600
That there are foreign armies that are poised to attack at any moment, that there are foreign

110
00:06:36,600 --> 00:06:38,660
sponsored bands of brigands.

111
00:06:39,900 --> 00:06:41,740
People attack foreigners.

112
00:06:41,840 --> 00:06:42,580
They attack Jews.

113
00:06:43,480 --> 00:06:44,880
They're looking for scapegoats.

114
00:06:45,000 --> 00:06:49,760
And this sort of mad paranoia that there's somebody out there, maybe just over the hill,

115
00:06:49,880 --> 00:06:53,980
who's going to attack your town, burn your crops, steal all your food, all of that kind

116
00:06:53,980 --> 00:06:54,340
of stuff.

117
00:06:54,560 --> 00:06:57,520
And so the process of people then forming militias to guard your town.

118
00:06:57,520 --> 00:07:08,260
And so it's a kind of knock-on effect that people are raising militias against the phantoms

119
00:07:08,260 --> 00:07:08,780
of militias.

120
00:07:08,860 --> 00:07:10,140
And so it goes on.

121
00:07:10,260 --> 00:07:13,160
And this is absolutely not what people want.

122
00:07:13,300 --> 00:07:18,800
You know, people are not demanding a kind of the disintegration of the country.

123
00:07:18,880 --> 00:07:20,560
Actually, they want greater control.

124
00:07:20,780 --> 00:07:24,640
They want people who can sort this out and give them bread and give them security.

125
00:07:26,540 --> 00:07:27,500
And basically, what they're trying to do is they're trying to make it so that they don't

126
00:07:27,500 --> 00:07:30,300
even know what they're doing.

127
00:07:34,880 --> 00:07:37,200
And it's going to be an attack on their country.

128
00:07:37,200 --> 00:07:40,500
And so, now, they are Lysa' hardly afraid to attack Hallo, I don't know if they can

129
00:07:40,500 --> 00:07:47,240
And so, what happens is that people are doing this kind of Believable movement, as Lysa'

130
00:07:47,360 --> 00:07:48,420
had said, they're doing this as a sort of threat to the people.

131
00:07:48,420 --> 00:07:48,660
So, it's like, you know, what are we waiting for?

132
00:07:48,660 --> 00:07:48,720
What are we waiting for today?

133
00:07:48,720 --> 00:07:49,360
What are we waiting for in the next week or so?

134
00:07:49,360 --> 00:07:54,200
And so, it's a kind of kind of security.

135
00:07:54,200 --> 00:07:55,540
It's like, what do we want, what are we waiting for?

136
00:07:55,700 --> 00:07:55,780
It's like level two.

137
00:07:56,520 --> 00:07:57,100
No, it's not.

138
00:07:57,100 --> 00:08:01,100
1989. And it's a time when the National Assembly is actually very thinly attended.

139
00:08:01,500 --> 00:08:06,580
It's the evening. And a guy stands up who you mentioned, Dominic, in the previous episode,

140
00:08:06,720 --> 00:08:11,780
the Vicomte de Neu, the guy who comes in and reports the fall of the Bastille to the king.

141
00:08:12,300 --> 00:08:17,160
And he's a man who has a foot in both the kind of the royal and the revolutionary camps.

142
00:08:17,400 --> 00:08:23,200
So his mother is Madame Etiquette, who is the, you know, the guide to Marie Antoinette,

143
00:08:23,200 --> 00:08:27,320
who is always kind of scolding her for breaking the etiquette and so on.

144
00:08:27,640 --> 00:08:31,300
So the upholder, the embodiment of the rules and regulations of the court.

145
00:08:32,320 --> 00:08:38,880
Exactly. But he has served alongside Lafayette, who is his brother-in-law in the American Revolution.

146
00:08:39,300 --> 00:08:45,600
And when Cornwallis surrendered at Yorktown, he had been the official representative of France.

147
00:08:46,080 --> 00:08:46,980
I hate him already.

148
00:08:47,900 --> 00:08:51,860
Right. Well, so he's interestingly positioned.

149
00:08:52,300 --> 00:08:53,180
And so he...

150
00:08:53,200 --> 00:08:59,780
He stands up and he says that the kingdom of France is floating between the complete destruction of society

151
00:08:59,780 --> 00:09:02,900
and a government which would be admired and followed throughout Europe.

152
00:09:03,140 --> 00:09:08,920
So in other words, we have to do something radical or it will be complete anarchy.

153
00:09:09,200 --> 00:09:14,160
And so the radical step he proposes is that all feudal obligations should be abolished,

154
00:09:15,040 --> 00:09:18,240
which, of course, is essentially means that from that point on,

155
00:09:18,300 --> 00:09:21,640
people will not need to go around ransacking chateaus and abbeys.

156
00:09:21,740 --> 00:09:23,180
And the other thing which he...

157
00:09:23,200 --> 00:09:28,080
He proposes in a very kind of offhand manner is that everyone in the kingdom should pay taxes.

158
00:09:28,260 --> 00:09:37,700
And this is precisely the issue that had been inspiring people in the previous year to refuse to accept the king's kind of fiscal solution.

159
00:09:37,940 --> 00:09:39,100
This is the maddest thing, isn't it?

160
00:09:39,200 --> 00:09:40,960
This is the maddest aspect of this whole story.

161
00:09:41,040 --> 00:09:48,040
This sense of, I mean, people talk about, one delegate I see describes it as a moment of patriotic drunkenness.

162
00:09:48,060 --> 00:09:51,880
People throwing their distinctions on the bonfire and saying, yeah, let's all pay tax.

163
00:09:51,880 --> 00:10:01,820
And the madness is this whole situation would not have come about if they had just agreed to pay tax as the king wanted a year or two earlier.

164
00:10:01,940 --> 00:10:09,580
I mean, it is the extent to which they have swung and they're now seized with this almost hysterical, almost paranoid passion.

165
00:10:10,160 --> 00:10:13,100
I mean, it really is one of the great political turnarounds.

166
00:10:13,220 --> 00:10:15,500
Yeah, so Sharma calls it a cult of self-dispossession.

167
00:10:15,740 --> 00:10:20,440
And I mean, I think it is something that would be very recognizable today.

168
00:10:20,540 --> 00:10:21,860
It's people checking.

169
00:10:21,880 --> 00:10:26,020
They're privilege, you know, standing up and saying, I have too much privilege.

170
00:10:26,260 --> 00:10:27,160
I must get rid of it all.

171
00:10:28,500 --> 00:10:35,580
But there's also, I mean, there's kind of tit for tat quality between the, say, the representatives of the church and the aristocracy.

172
00:10:36,040 --> 00:10:40,220
So a bishop stand up and proposes the abolition of all hunting rights.

173
00:10:40,500 --> 00:10:44,080
So essentially from this point on, anyone can just shoot anything that they want.

174
00:10:44,280 --> 00:10:48,460
And so Duke then stands up and says, yeah, fine, but we'll get rid of church tithes.

175
00:10:49,060 --> 00:10:51,860
And by the end of it, I think it's not just feudalism.

176
00:10:51,880 --> 00:10:55,720
That's been jettisoned, but the very notion of privilege itself.

177
00:10:57,460 --> 00:11:05,440
And so William Doyle in the Oxford history of the French Revolution, he says it proved to be the most sweeping and radical legislative session of the whole French Revolution.

178
00:11:05,740 --> 00:11:10,440
And it was all just the event of an evening, a night, people standing up and saying, let's get rid of everything.

179
00:11:10,580 --> 00:11:15,140
All that fuss about the defense of privilege and privilege being part of liberty.

180
00:11:15,420 --> 00:11:17,840
And now in one evening, they're throwing them all away.

181
00:11:18,060 --> 00:11:19,860
Yeah, it's amazing.

182
00:11:20,500 --> 00:11:21,320
And then.

183
00:11:21,880 --> 00:11:29,940
Of course, three weeks later comes the declaration, which is the second kind of monumental moment in this process of legislation.

184
00:11:31,820 --> 00:11:37,560
And you said yes last time that actually the guy who originally proposes it is your mate Lafayette.

185
00:11:37,820 --> 00:11:42,060
An empty headed political dwarf, as the dictionary of the French Revolution calls him.

186
00:11:42,120 --> 00:11:46,100
A dashing representative of Franco-American friendship.

187
00:11:47,000 --> 00:11:48,940
And obviously he's been in America.

188
00:11:49,060 --> 00:11:50,900
He's very close to George Washington.

189
00:11:50,900 --> 00:11:56,300
He has close relations with Thomas Jefferson, who is the American ambassador in Paris at this point.

190
00:11:56,860 --> 00:12:00,960
And so he basically proposes that it be on the American model.

191
00:12:01,120 --> 00:12:11,380
And he thinks, you know, just as the American declaration had joined the various states in America together, he thinks that it will help unify a sundering nation in France.

192
00:12:11,620 --> 00:12:13,680
And it proclaims 17 rights.

193
00:12:14,100 --> 00:12:19,400
And the first of these, men are born and remain free and equal in rights.

194
00:12:19,480 --> 00:12:20,780
Social distinctions may be based.

195
00:12:20,900 --> 00:12:22,740
Only on considerations of the common good.

196
00:12:23,000 --> 00:12:24,360
So that is egalite.

197
00:12:25,000 --> 00:12:28,900
That's the expression of all the feudal privileges that have been jettisoned.

198
00:12:28,900 --> 00:12:33,120
And this, in turn, then defines what the purpose of good governance should be.

199
00:12:33,300 --> 00:12:37,980
The aim of every political association is the preservation of the natural and imprescriptible rights of man.

200
00:12:38,140 --> 00:12:41,860
These rights are liberty, property, safety and resistance to oppression.

201
00:12:42,160 --> 00:12:44,580
With an emphasis there, I think, on the first word, liberte.

202
00:12:44,780 --> 00:12:45,260
Yes.

203
00:12:45,300 --> 00:12:46,400
So that's the second article.

204
00:12:46,500 --> 00:12:49,740
And I think it's very obvious what the target of that article is.

205
00:12:49,880 --> 00:12:50,880
And the third article is.

206
00:12:50,900 --> 00:13:00,780
The third article makes it clear, the principle of any sovereignty lies primarily in the nation, no corporate body, no individual may exercise any authority that does not expressly emanate from it.

207
00:13:00,820 --> 00:13:07,640
So the corporate body is clearly the church and the individual is clearly the king, although neither are actually mentioned.

208
00:13:07,840 --> 00:13:09,780
And no supernatural authority there either.

209
00:13:09,900 --> 00:13:11,240
No supernatural authority, yeah.

210
00:13:11,320 --> 00:13:20,840
But interestingly, the nation, so a new abstract noun, no one questions what the nation might be or says, well, actually, that's quite hard to define.

211
00:13:20,840 --> 00:13:20,880
Well, actually, that's quite hard to define.

212
00:13:20,880 --> 00:13:22,680
But who belongs to the nation?

213
00:13:22,800 --> 00:13:23,820
What does the nation even mean?

214
00:13:23,980 --> 00:13:27,280
Well, the nation is a community joined in the bonds of fraternity.

215
00:13:27,420 --> 00:13:27,820
Yeah.

216
00:13:27,920 --> 00:13:32,020
So we have galate, liberte, fraternity.

217
00:13:32,260 --> 00:13:34,880
They're all they've all been kind of name checked.

218
00:13:35,580 --> 00:13:40,560
And then so that's the kind of the more abstract end of the list.

219
00:13:40,840 --> 00:13:50,700
But what you then have is a whole list of articles that are clearly very, very prescriptively trying to knock away the various.

220
00:13:50,880 --> 00:13:52,460
props of the Ancien Regime.

221
00:13:52,800 --> 00:13:56,560
So Article 9, every man is presumed innocent until he's been declared guilty.

222
00:13:57,000 --> 00:14:02,820
That's targeting the letter to cachet, the way in which the king could just have people arrested and put in prison.

223
00:14:03,740 --> 00:14:11,920
Article 10, no one may be disturbed on account of his opinions, even religious ones, as long as the manifestation of such opinions does not interfere with the established law and order.

224
00:14:12,080 --> 00:14:16,860
So that's targeting the privileged position held by the Catholic Church.

225
00:14:17,160 --> 00:14:17,880
This is in law.

226
00:14:18,080 --> 00:14:20,860
So ever since 1715, the Protestants.

